# bix-51-q5 — BMI vs treatment response (logistic regression)

**Quoted answer: Wald p-value for BMI = 0.0865** (z = 1.714, two-sided).
The BMI null is **not rejected at alpha = 0.05**; direction is positive — higher BMI is
associated with higher odds of response (OR 1.183 per 1 kg/m2, 95% CI 0.976–1.433).

Headline model (unadjusted, as confirmed): `response ~ BMI`, N = 80, MLE (statsmodels Logit),
intercept = -3.631934 (SE 2.1593), beta_BMI = 0.167746 (SE 0.097869).

Concordance / robustness on the same null: LR chi2(1) = 3.0411, p = 0.08118;
score test p = 0.08292; GLM/IRLS p = 0.086530; hand-rolled Newton MLE p = 0.086529518
(agrees to 1.2e-12); profile-likelihood 95% CI for beta [-0.0205, 0.3660].
Descriptive cross-checks: mean BMI 22.407 (responders) vs 21.490 (non-responders),
Welch p = 0.0854, Mann-Whitney p = 0.0638.

Sensitivity only (NOT the answer): BMI quartiles joint LR p = 0.2139 (3 df);
non-linearity LR p = 0.4866 (2 df); adjusted (age, sex, ECOG-PS, Child-Pugh) BMI term
beta = 0.1699, Wald p = 0.1436. `Groups` excluded — complete separation with the outcome
(Group 1 = 41 PR / 0 else; Group 2 = 0 PR / 39 SD+PD), demonstrated empirically in section 4b.

Limitations: Efficacy is a 3-level ordinal (PR 41 / SD 17 / PD 22) collapsed to binary;
BMI units assumed kg/m2; n = 80 with 41/39 events.

Reproduce: `python3 analysis/bmi_logistic_regression.py` (log: analysis/bmi_logistic_regression.out;
tables: analysis/results_table.csv, analysis/quartile_calibration.csv).
